      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.346,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.469,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": "Consider the numbers 1, 8, 27, ..."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.107,
            0.848,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "These numbers are called **perfect cubes.** Can you see why they are named so?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.143,
            0.848,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": "Each of them is obtained by multiplying a number by itself three times. We note that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.439,
            0.186,
            0.559,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": "1 = 1 \\times 1 \\times 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.438,
            0.209,
            0.56,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": "8 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.233,
            0.565,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": "2 7 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 3",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.26,
            0.272,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": "Is 9 a cube?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.286,
            0.738,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "We see that $2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 8$ and $3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 2 7$ . This shows that 9 is not a perfect cube. Nor is any number from 10 to 26."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.746,
            0.204,
            0.941,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.334,
            0.736,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "? Can you estimate the number of unit cubes in a cube with an edge length of 4 units?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.378,
            0.682,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": "It has 64 unit cubes! If you notice carefully, each layer of this cube has $4 \\times 4$ unit cubes. Each square layer has 16 unit cubes $( 4 \\times 4 )$ , and there are 4 such layers, so the total number of unit cubes is $4 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 64.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.451,
            0.527,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "Since $5^{3} = 5 \\times 5 \\times 5 = 125,$ , 125 is a cube."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.47,
            0.681,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "**In general, for any number n, we write the cube** $\\pmb { n } \\times \\pmb { n } \\times \\pmb { n }$ as $n ^ { 3 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.686,
            0.361,
            0.942,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.517,
            0.407,
            0.54
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Complete the table below."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.29,
            0.554,
            0.347,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "1 ^ { 3 } = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.441,
            0.554,
            0.544,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "1 1 ^ { 3 } = 1 3 3 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.289,
            0.579,
            0.349,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "2^{3} = 8",
          "image_source": "images/page_11_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.441,
            0.58,
